  "headline": "v1 (deliberately worse: vague rules, no anti-injection warning, minimal answer schema, thin tool docs) scored 27.1% (29/107); v2 (explicit check order, anti-injection warning, fixed trigger/missing schema, full tool descriptors, evidence-enriched) scored 100.0% (107/107) on the identical 107 trials, same model. v1 substantially and deliberately worse than v2, as intended.",
  "interpretation": "This is a genuinely different, more informative comparison than the old 35-case one. The old set was 82% positive/'book', so v1's lack of an exact-label schema rarely mattered - most cases just needed a correct booking, which v1's tool use (unaffected by the poka-yokes) handled fine. The new set is 69% negative, split across FIVE distinct trigger types (red_flag_term, specialty_mismatch, duplicate_future_appointment, no_slot_in_window, instruction_in_referral_free_text) plus request_information's exact missing-string format - and v1 has no way to know these five labels exist at all. v1 still reaches the RIGHT underlying decision on most of these (escalate vs book vs request_information), it just cannot name the trigger/missing field correctly without v2's schema - see results/descriptors/v1_live.json's `failures` field, where the vast majority of v1's 78 failures are exactly this pattern, not wrong decisions or wrong bookings."
